<BaseLayout title="About the project" description="Read the mission, editorial principles, international translation plans, and public participation goals behind Whaling Watch.">
  <section class="container closing-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Whaling Watch</p>
    <h2>Observe first. Explain clearly. Cite the source.</h2>
    <p>Whaling Watch is an independent, international information project about contemporary whaling. We document the practice without treating a country, community, or individual claim as shorthand for the whole subject.</p>
    <p>Our aim is simple: make reliable context easier to reach—across commercial activity, Aboriginal and subsistence whaling, law, science, culture, and the public record.</p>
    <div class="mission-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">The mission</p>
      <h3>Make the international picture understandable—and make it available internationally.</h3>
      <p>Whaling Watch exists to document who whales, where, why, under which laws, and with what available evidence. We do not begin with a predetermined verdict. We separate categories that are too often collapsed together, make disagreement and uncertainty visible, and provide a path back to the original source.</p>
      <p>English is the starting point, not the intended boundary. The project is being structured for future translations relevant to the places and communities it covers, with human review before any translation is treated as authoritative.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="participate-panel">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">A public watch</p><h3>Help us see what we missed.</h3></div>
      <div><p>Researchers, coastal communities, photographers, translators, journalists, and careful readers are invited to share sources, context, corrections, and original material. Participation does not require agreement—only good faith and a willingness to show the evidence.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="/contact">Contribute to the project →</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="artist-credit">
      <p class="eyebrow">Artwork</p>
      <h3>Angga gave Whaling Watch its inhabitants.</h3>
      <p>Angga’s original whale illustrations bring warmth, curiosity, and a wonderfully strange personality to a subject that is too often presented without either. Their work helps this project feel inviting without weakening its seriousness.</p>
      <p>If you enjoy the whales swimming through this site, please <a href="https://vgen.co/AnggaPokoke/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">visit Angga on VGen</a>, follow their work, or consider supporting them with a commission of your own.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
